{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiEsQiT6KQbF3GvsAmbn4R","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KA\n4\t**CU:** C03 — Manual Body Massage | **Duration:** 1 Hour | **Total Marks:** 100\n5\t**Paper Colour:** PINK\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Section A: MCQ (20 marks — 2 each)\n10\t\n11\t**1.** Effleurage strokes should always move: A. Away from heart B. Towards heart C. In circular motion D. In any direction\n12\t**2.** Which movement is used to break down adhesions? A. Effleurage B. Vibration C. Friction D. Tapotement\n13\t**3.** An absolute contraindication for massage: A. Mild eczema B. DVT C. Controlled diabetes D. Low BP\n14\t**4.** The therapist should apply pressure using: A. Arm strength B. Body weight transfer C. Wrist flexion D. Finger strength\n15\t**5.** Tapotement includes all EXCEPT: A. Hacking B. Cupping C. Kneading D. Beating\n16\t**6.** Suitable oil for oily skin: A. Coconut B. Sweet almond C. Grapeseed D. Olive\n17\t**7.** Post-massage soreness normally lasts: A. 2-4 hrs B. 24-48 hrs C. 5-7 days D. 2 weeks\n18\t**8.** Towels must be laundered at minimum: A. 40 C B. 50 C C. 60 C D. 80 C\n19\t**9.** Continuity in massage means: A. Increasing speed B. Maintaining hand contact C. Using same pressure D. Using one movement\n20\t**10.** Side-lying position is recommended for: A. Back massage B. Pregnant clients C. Arm massage D. Foot massage\n21\t\n22\t## Section B: True or False (20 marks — 4 each)\n23\t**11.** Friction requires generous application of massage oil.\n24\t**12.** Petrissage improves tissue elasticity and breaks down adhesions.\n25\t**13.** The therapist should lock their knees for stability during massage.\n26\t**14.** Client consent is only needed for the first massage session.\n27\t**15.** Vibration has a soothing effect on nerve endings.\n28\t\n29\t## Section C: Short Answer (30 marks)\n30\t**16.** Describe the five classical massage movements with their effects. (15 marks)\n31\t**17.** List FIVE (5) principles of proper body mechanics. (5 marks)\n32\t**18.** A client reports areas of tension in the upper back and shoulders. Describe the massage sequence for this area, including which movements you would use and why. (10 marks)\n33\t\n34\t## Section D: Structured (30 marks)\n35\t**19.** A 38-year-old office worker requests a full-body relaxation massage. She has mild varicose veins on the left calf and reports shoulder tension from desk work.\n36\ta) Identify contraindication(s) and state the action. (6 marks)\n37\tb) Select an appropriate massage medium and justify. (4 marks)\n38\tc) Outline the full-body massage flow sequence for this client, noting any modifications for her condition. (10 marks)\n39\td) List FIVE (5) after care advice points. (5 marks)\n40\te) What must be recorded after the session? (5 marks)\n41\t\n42\t---\n43\t\n44\t## Marking Scheme\n45\t\n46\t### Section A\n47\t1-B, 2-C, 3-B, 4-B, 5-C, 6-C, 7-B, 8-C, 9-B, 10-B\n48\t\n49\t### Section B\n50\t**11.** FALSE — Friction requires minimal or no medium. **12.** TRUE. **13.** FALSE — Knees should be slightly bent. **14.** FALSE — Consent needed for every session. **15.** TRUE.\n51\t\n52\t### Section C\n53\t**16.** 3 marks per movement: name, technique description, effects. **17.** 1 mark each: wide stance, bent knees, straight back, relaxed shoulders, body weight transfer. **18.** Start effleurage (warm tissue), petrissage on trapezius/rhomboids (release tension), friction on specific knots (break adhesions), tapotement if desired (stimulate), close effleurage (calm). Justify each choice.\n54\t\n55\t### Section D\n56\t**19a.** Varicose veins (left calf): Relative. Avoid direct pressure on affected area; massage above/below; use light strokes over area. **19b.** Sweet almond oil — light, hypoallergenic, good slip for relaxation massage. **19c.** Standard flow but avoid deep pressure/tapotement on left calf; additional focus on neck/shoulders with petrissage and friction. **19d.** Any 5: drink water, rest, avoid exercise 24h, expect mild soreness, warm bath, avoid heavy meals, note continued discomfort. **19e.** Treatment performed, areas worked (including modification for varicose veins), medium used, client response, pressure used, after care given, date/signature.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"3a92ade5-1ca3-4f3c-aca7-1b71ab0ae8ed","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:54:27.686Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KA.md","content":"# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KA\n**CU:** C03 — Manual Body Massage | **Duration:** 1 Hour | **Total Marks:** 100\n**Paper Colour:** PINK\n\n---\n\n## Section A: MCQ (20 marks — 2 each)\n\n**1.** Effleurage strokes should always move: A. Away from heart B. Towards heart C. In circular motion D. In any direction\n**2.** Which movement is used to break down adhesions? A. Effleurage B. Vibration C. Friction D. Tapotement\n**3.** An absolute contraindication for massage: A. Mild eczema B. DVT C. Controlled diabetes D. Low BP\n**4.** The therapist should apply pressure using: A. Arm strength B. Body weight transfer C. Wrist flexion D. Finger strength\n**5.** Tapotement includes all EXCEPT: A. Hacking B. Cupping C. Kneading D. Beating\n**6.** Suitable oil for oily skin: A. Coconut B. Sweet almond C. Grapeseed D. Olive\n**7.** Post-massage soreness normally lasts: A. 2-4 hrs B. 24-48 hrs C. 5-7 days D. 2 weeks\n**8.** Towels must be laundered at minimum: A. 40 C B. 50 C C. 60 C D. 80 C\n**9.** Continuity in massage means: A. Increasing speed B. Maintaining hand contact C. Using same pressure D. Using one movement\n**10.** Side-lying position is recommended for: A. Back massage B. Pregnant clients C. Arm massage D. Foot massage\n\n## Section B: True or False (20 marks — 4 each)\n**11.** Friction requires generous application of massage oil.\n**12.** Petrissage improves tissue elasticity and breaks down adhesions.\n**13.** The therapist should lock their knees for stability during massage.\n**14.** Client consent is only needed for the first massage session.\n**15.** Vibration has a soothing effect on nerve endings.\n\n## Section C: Short Answer (30 marks)\n**16.** Describe the five classical massage movements with their effects. (15 marks)\n**17.** List FIVE (5) principles of proper body mechanics. (5 marks)\n**18.** A client reports areas of tension in the upper back and shoulders. Describe the massage sequence for this area, including which movements you would use and why. (10 marks)\n\n## Section D: Structured (30 marks)\n**19.** A 38-year-old office worker requests a full-body relaxation massage. She has mild varicose veins on the left calf and reports shoulder tension from desk work.\na) Identify contraindication(s) and state the action. (6 marks)\nb) Select an appropriate massage medium and justify. (4 marks)\nc) Outline the full-body massage flow sequence for this client, noting any modifications for her condition. (10 marks)\nd) List FIVE (5) after care advice points. (5 marks)\ne) What must be recorded after the session? (5 marks)\n\n---\n\n## Marking Scheme\n\n### Section A\n1-B, 2-C, 3-B, 4-B, 5-C, 6-C, 7-B, 8-C, 9-B, 10-B\n\n### Section B\n**11.** FALSE — Friction requires minimal or no medium. **12.** TRUE. **13.** FALSE — Knees should be slightly bent. **14.** FALSE — Consent needed for every session. **15.** TRUE.\n\n### Section C\n**16.** 3 marks per movement: name, technique description, effects. **17.** 1 mark each: wide stance, bent knees, straight back, relaxed shoulders, body weight transfer. **18.** Start effleurage (warm tissue), petrissage on trapezius/rhomboids (release tension), friction on specific knots (break adhesions), tapotement if desired (stimulate), close effleurage (calm). Justify each choice.\n\n### Section D\n**19a.** Varicose veins (left calf): Relative. Avoid direct pressure on affected area; massage above/below; use light strokes over area. **19b.** Sweet almond oil — light, hypoallergenic, good slip for relaxation massage. **19c.** Standard flow but avoid deep pressure/tapotement on left calf; additional focus on neck/shoulders with petrissage and friction. **19d.** Any 5: drink water, rest, avoid exercise 24h, expect mild soreness, warm bath, avoid heavy meals, note continued discomfort. **19e.** Treatment performed, areas worked (including modification for varicose veins), medium used, client response, pressure used, after care given, date/signature.\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Knm2ceokMtCfnYL8h7uBe3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The KA.md already exists in aesthetic-services/C03/. Let me check the story more carefully.\n\nThe story says: \"File aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md created with 10 distinct questions...\"\n\nBut the existing file is at `aesthetic-services/C03/KA.md`, and the directory is `C03` not `c03-manual-body-massage`.\n\nThe existing KA.md has questions but it's structured differently from what the story requires. The story needs:\n- 10 questions total: 4 anatomy identification, 3 scenario-based technique selection, 2 contraindication identification, 1 safety procedure\n\n\n- Each question needs: answer scheme with rubric points (basic/intermediate/advanced confidence levels)\n- Bilingual key terms with EN:/BM: format\n- CoCU Reference codes\n- Scenario questions need 2-3 point rubric\n\nThe current KA.md is a different format (MCQ, T/F, Short Answer, Structured). The story wants a specific 10-question format with anatomy, scenario, contraindication, and safety questions. There's also a mismatch between the test expecting `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md` and the actual structure using `aesthetic-services/C03/`.\n\nLooking at the test commands, they're checking for exactly 10 questions, at least 3 scenario or case study questions, and at least 30 bilingual terms total across the file. The directory path `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/` doesn't exist yet, so I need to create it with the KA.md file matching what the tests expect. The acceptance criteria specify `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md`, but the existing project structure uses `aesthetic-services/C03/`. I need to figure out whether to create a new directory with the lowercase naming convention or update the existing uppercase one. Let me check what format the existing KA.md uses and whether it matches the required 10-question structure, since that might clarify the intent.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2620,"cache_read_input_tokens":32914,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2620},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"7f55d414-f03b-42b4-8f48-f848f38227fd"}
