{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WbV3NhsCAXXLRdNWyKfJ7V","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C03/KA\n4\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n9\t\n10\t| Field | Detail |\n11\t|-------|--------|\n12\t| Duration | 1 hour 30 minutes |\n13\t| Total Marks | 100 |\n14\t| Passing Mark | 60% (60 marks) |\n15\t| References | KP(1/3), KP(2/3), KP(3/3) |\n16\t\n17\t**Instructions:** Answer ALL questions. Write clearly. Marks for each question are shown in brackets.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## BAHAGIAN A — PENGETAHUAN TEORI / THEORETICAL KNOWLEDGE (30 marks)\n22\t\n23\t## Question 1 (10 marks)\n24\t\n25\tA BEV typically has multiple separate cooling circuits unlike a single-circuit ICE vehicle. Describe THREE (3) cooling circuits found in a modern BEV by stating: (a) the circuit name, (b) the components it cools, and (c) the operating temperature range maintained by the Thermal Management ECU.\n26\t\n27\t---\n28\t\n29\t## Question 2 (10 marks)\n30\t\n31\tDescribe the function and operation of the following five (5) BEV thermal management components. For each, state whether it is connected to the Low Voltage (LV) or High Voltage (HV) system:\n32\t\n33\t(a) Electric water pump (b) PTC heater (c) 3-way valve (d) Electric compressor (e) Electronic expansion valve (EXV)\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## Question 3 (10 marks)\n38\t\n39\t(a) Explain why BEV liquid cooling systems may use **dielectric coolant** instead of standard ethylene glycol. State TWO (2) consequences of mixing dielectric coolant with standard coolant. *(6 marks)*\n40\t\n41\t(b) Classify R1234yf refrigerant by safety group and state ONE (1) handling requirement resulting from this classification. *(4 marks)*\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## BAHAGIAN B — DIAGNOSTIK SENARIO / DIAGNOSTIC SCENARIOS (70 marks)\n46\t\n47\t## Question 4 (10 marks)\n48\t\n49\t**System State:** A BEV is being DC fast-charged at a public charger. The Thermal Management ECU activates the battery cooling circuit. Battery temperature rises to 45 °C (manufacturer limit: 40 °C). Fault code **P0A93 — High Voltage Battery Pack Temperature** is stored. The charger automatically reduces charging rate to 20% of maximum.\n50\t\n51\t**Symptoms:** Battery over-temperature MIL active; charging slower than expected; electric water pump runs continuously.\n52\t\n53\t**Required:** (a) List FOUR (4) possible causes of the battery cooling failure in the liquid cooling circuit. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe the FIRST FOUR (4) diagnostic steps a technician should perform, in correct sequence. *(6 marks)*\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## Question 5 (10 marks)\n58\t\n59\t**System State:** Ambient temperature 35 °C. Driver operates HVAC on maximum cooling (set: 18 °C). Refrigerant high-side pressure reads 28 bar; low-side reads 1.2 bar. Normal high-side should be 16–22 bar at this ambient temperature.\n60\t\n61\t**Symptoms:** Cabin vent temperature 24 °C (should be 5–10 °C); compressor makes clicking/cycling noise every 30 seconds; no refrigerant leaks visible.\n62\t\n63\t**Required:** (a) Interpret the abnormal refrigerant pressures and identify the MOST LIKELY cause. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe the diagnostic procedure to confirm your diagnosis and state the corrective action. *(6 marks)*\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## Question 6 (10 marks)\n68\t\n69\t**System State:** A BEV parked overnight. Next morning, the driver notices a clear puddle under the front of the vehicle. Low Coolant Level warning illuminates on startup. Expansion tank level is at MIN mark.\n70\t\n71\t**Symptoms:** No fault codes stored; electric water pump runs normally; no steam from under bonnet; coolant appears colourless and clean.\n72\t\n73\t**Required:** (a) List the THREE (3) most likely sources of a BEV coolant leak in this scenario. *(3 marks)* (b) Describe the complete leak detection procedure using a coolant pressure tester, including: preparation, pressurising step, acceptable pressure hold time, and interpretation of results. *(7 marks)*\n74\t\n75\t---\n76\t\n77\t## Question 7 (8 marks)\n78\t\n79\t**System State:** Battery temperature rises to 38 °C during highway driving (limit 40 °C). Technician connects scan tool and performs active test on the HV battery cooling water pump. Scan tool shows: Commanded = ON; Current draw = 0.2 A (normal: 4–8 A); No rotation detected.\n80\t\n81\t**Symptoms:** High battery temperature; active test fails; no coolant flow noise from battery circuit.\n82\t\n83\t**Required:** Diagnose whether the fault is in (a) the water pump itself, (b) the pump drive circuit (wiring/fuse), or (c) the Thermal Management ECU. For each, state ONE (1) specific test to confirm or eliminate it as the fault source. *(8 marks)*\n84\t\n85\t---\n86\t\n87\t## Question 8 (8 marks)\n88\t\n89\t**System State:** After replacing the electric water pump on the battery cooling circuit, the technician must verify the repair is complete and the system is functioning correctly before returning the vehicle.\n90\t\n91\t**Required:** Describe the complete **cooling system functionality test** procedure after water pump replacement. Include: coolant refill, air bleeding, active test, temperature monitoring, and final verification steps. State the acceptable temperature range for the battery circuit at idle. *(8 marks)*\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## Question 9 (4 marks)\n96\t\n97\t**System State:** A technician is about to replace the electric compressor on a BEV. The vehicle uses R1234yf refrigerant.\n98\t\n99\t**Required:** State the mandatory refrigerant recovery procedure that must be completed BEFORE removing the compressor. Include: (a) the equipment used, (b) the reason for recovery (not venting), and (c) the HV safety step required for compressor removal. *(4 marks)*\n100\t\n101\t---\n102\t\n103\t## Question 10 (10 marks)\n104\t\n105\t**System State:** A scan tool reveals THREE simultaneous fault codes on a BEV brought in for cooling complaints:\n106\t- **P0A93** — Battery Pack Over-Temperature\n107\t- **P0AAD** — Battery Coolant Pump A Circuit Low\n108\t- **B1678** — HVAC Refrigerant Pressure Sensor Circuit\n109\t\n110\t**Symptoms:** Battery temperature: 42 °C; HVAC cabin temp: 26 °C (set: 22 °C); no coolant visible on ground.\n111\t\n112\t**Required:** (a) Explain how you would PRIORITISE these three fault codes for diagnosis and why. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe a systematic diagnostic approach to determine whether the faults are independent or related to a single root cause. *(6 marks)*\n113\t\n114\t---\n115\t\n116\t## Question 11 (10 marks)\n117\t\n118\t**System State:** A 5-year-old BEV (odometer: 95,000 km) is brought in for annual service. The owner reports that fast charging now causes the battery temperature to reach 38 °C, whereas it used to stabilise at 30–32 °C when the vehicle was new. No fault codes are stored.\n119\t\n120\t**Symptoms:** Gradual performance degradation of battery cooling; all cooling components function during active test; coolant level correct; electric water pump current normal.\n121\t\n122\t**Required:** (a) List THREE (3) possible causes of gradual cooling performance degradation in an aging BEV. *(3 marks)* (b) Describe the inspection procedure to identify the root cause, including coolant condition check, clogging inspection, and heat exchanger assessment. *(7 marks)*\n123\t\n124\t---\n125\t\n126\t## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n127\t\n128\t### Question 1 (10 marks — 3–4 marks per circuit; deduct for incomplete descriptions)\n129\t\n130\t| Circuit | Components | Temperature Range |\n131\t|---------|-----------|-------------------|\n132\t| Battery cooling | Traction battery pack | 15–35 °C (precisely controlled) |\n133\t| Powertrain cooling | Motor, inverter, DC-DC converter | Up to 65 °C |\n134\t| HVAC (cabin) | Condenser, evaporator, PTC heater | Per climate demand |\n135\t\n136\t**Rubric per circuit:** 4 = name + components + temp range correct; 3 = two elements correct; 2 = one element correct; 1 = vague answer.\n137\t\n138\t### Question 2 (10 marks — 2 marks each)\n139\t\n140\t(a) Electric water pump: circulates coolant independently of motor speed; can run stationary; **HV-powered** (battery/powertrain circuit) or **LV** (auxiliary).\n141\t(b) PTC heater: electric resistance cabin heater, self-regulating, **HV-powered**, 3–8 kW.\n142\t(c) 3-way valve: directs coolant between circuits; **LV** controlled by Thermal ECU.\n143\t(d) Electric compressor: compresses refrigerant for HVAC cooling; **HV-powered**.\n144\t(e) EXV: regulates refrigerant flow/pressure at evaporator inlet; **LV** (electronically controlled).\n145\t\n146\t### Question 3 (10 marks)\n147\t\n148\t**(a) 6 marks:** Dielectric coolant prevents electrical short-circuit if coolant contacts HV components (2m). Mixing consequences: degraded dielectric properties leading to electrical conductivity risk (2m); possible corrosion/precipitation/gel from incompatible inhibitor packages (2m).\n149\t**(b) 4 marks:** R1234yf = A2L (mildly flammable, low toxicity) (2m). Handling requirement: store away from ignition sources / use approved refrigerant recovery machine rated for A2L / no open flames in work area (2m).\n150\t\n151\t### Question 4 (10 marks)\n152\t\n153\t**(a) 4 marks — 1 mark each:** Water pump failure; clogged coolant passage/heat exchanger; low coolant level (leak); thermostat/3-way valve stuck; degraded coolant (reduced thermal transfer).\n154\t**(b) 6 marks — 1.5 marks each step:** (1) Read and record all fault codes; (2) Check coolant level in expansion tank; (3) Perform active test on battery cooling pump — verify current and flow; (4) Pressure test cooling circuit for leaks.\n155\t\n156\t### Question 5 (10 marks)\n157\t\n158\t**(a) 4 marks:** High-side 28 bar = significantly above normal (16–22 bar) → over-charged system or blocked condenser (2m). Low-side 1.2 bar = normal/slightly low. Most likely: refrigerant overcharge OR condenser airflow blockage causing excessive head pressure (2m).\n159\t**(b) 6 marks:** Confirm condenser fan operation (1m); inspect condenser for blockage (leaves, debris) (1m); if condenser clear, measure refrigerant charge vs. manufacturer specification by weight (2m); if overcharged, recover excess refrigerant using recovery machine until correct charge weight achieved (2m).\n160\t\n161\t### Question 6 (10 marks)\n162\t\n163\t**(a) 3 marks — 1 each):** Coolant hose connection/fitting; expansion tank cap seal; heat exchanger (radiator) pinhole; water pump housing seal; coolant temperature sensor O-ring.\n164\t**(b) 7 marks:** Connect pressure tester to expansion tank neck (1m); pressurise to manufacturer specification — typically 1.0–1.5 bar (2m); observe pressure gauge for 10 minutes (1m); pressure drop indicates leak present (1m); inspect all hoses, fittings, pump, heat exchangers systematically while pressurised (2m); UV dye can supplement if leak not visible.\n165\t\n166\t### Question 7 (8 marks — 2–3 marks each)\n167\t\n168\t(a) **Pump itself:** Disconnect pump connector → measure resistance across pump motor terminals; out-of-spec = pump failed internally (3m).\n169\t(b) **Drive circuit:** With connector disconnected, measure voltage at supply terminal with pump commanded ON; 0V = blown fuse or open circuit wiring (3m).\n170\t(c) **Thermal ECU:** If supply voltage present and pump resistance in spec but no rotation → pump seized; if no command signal output → ECU fault; verify ECU output with oscilloscope or scan tool live data (2m).\n171\t\n172\t### Question 8 (8 marks)\n173\t\n174\tRefill coolant to MAX level (1m) → bleed air per manufacturer procedure (gravity fill or vacuum fill tool) (2m) → connect scan tool and run active test on water pump; verify current 4–8A and flow audible (2m) → start vehicle in READY mode; monitor coolant temperatures via scan tool (1m) → battery circuit temperature should stabilise at 20–30 °C at idle (1m) → verify no pressure decay on pressure test and no fault codes (1m).\n175\t\n176\t### Question 9 (4 marks)\n177\t\n178\t(a) Equipment: refrigerant recycling/recovery machine rated for R1234yf (1m).\n179\t(b) Reason: R1234yf is a greenhouse gas; venting is prohibited under environmental regulations; must be recovered for reuse or certified disposal (1m).\n180\t(c) HV safety: perform full HV de-energisation and confirm zero-energy state before disconnecting any compressor electrical connector or refrigerant lines — compressor drive operates at HV (up to 400V DC) (2m).\n181\t\n182\t### Question 10 (10 marks)\n183\t\n184\t**(a) 4 marks:** Prioritise P0AAD (pump circuit low) first — it is a root cause that directly leads to P0A93 (over-temp) (2m). B1678 (pressure sensor) is separate HVAC issue (1m). Correct prioritisation order: P0AAD → P0A93 (related) → B1678 (independent) (1m).\n185\t**(b) 6 marks:** Diagnose P0AAD first: active test battery pump, check fuse and wiring (2m); if pump fault confirmed and repaired, re-scan — if P0A93 clears, faults were related (2m); diagnose B1678 independently: check refrigerant pressure sensor wiring, connector, and sensor output voltage (2m).\n186\t\n187\t### Question 11 (10 marks)\n188\t\n189\t**(a) 3 marks — 1 each:** Degraded coolant (reduced thermal conductivity after years of service); internal clogging of battery heat exchanger (scale/particulate buildup); reduced electric water pump performance (wear, reduced flow rate).\n190\t**(b) 7 marks:** Coolant condition: check colour (should be clean, specified colour), pH with test strips (2m); clogging inspection: compare water pump flow rate (active test current draw vs. spec); check for particulate in coolant (2m); heat exchanger: inspect for external debris blockage; perform cooling circuit flush test; compare inlet vs. outlet temperature differential across heat exchanger — reduced differential indicates reduced heat transfer (3m).\n191\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"0416a4c2-7b88-4916-a842-cb5d5f554dbc","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:55:13.797Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\bev-diagnostic-rectification\\C03\\KA.md","content":"# PENILAIAN PENGETAHUAN / KNOWLEDGE ASSESSMENT\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C03/KA\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** MERAH JAMBU / PINK\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n\n| Field | Detail |\n|-------|--------|\n| Duration | 1 hour 30 minutes |\n| Total Marks | 100 |\n| Passing Mark | 60% (60 marks) |\n| References | KP(1/3), KP(2/3), KP(3/3) |\n\n**Instructions:** Answer ALL questions. Write clearly. Marks for each question are shown in brackets.\n\n---\n\n## BAHAGIAN A — PENGETAHUAN TEORI / THEORETICAL KNOWLEDGE (30 marks)\n\n## Question 1 (10 marks)\n\nA BEV typically has multiple separate cooling circuits unlike a single-circuit ICE vehicle. Describe THREE (3) cooling circuits found in a modern BEV by stating: (a) the circuit name, (b) the components it cools, and (c) the operating temperature range maintained by the Thermal Management ECU.\n\n---\n\n## Question 2 (10 marks)\n\nDescribe the function and operation of the following five (5) BEV thermal management components. For each, state whether it is connected to the Low Voltage (LV) or High Voltage (HV) system:\n\n(a) Electric water pump (b) PTC heater (c) 3-way valve (d) Electric compressor (e) Electronic expansion valve (EXV)\n\n---\n\n## Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n(a) Explain why BEV liquid cooling systems may use **dielectric coolant** instead of standard ethylene glycol. State TWO (2) consequences of mixing dielectric coolant with standard coolant. *(6 marks)*\n\n(b) Classify R1234yf refrigerant by safety group and state ONE (1) handling requirement resulting from this classification. *(4 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## BAHAGIAN B — DIAGNOSTIK SENARIO / DIAGNOSTIC SCENARIOS (70 marks)\n\n## Question 4 (10 marks)\n\n**System State:** A BEV is being DC fast-charged at a public charger. The Thermal Management ECU activates the battery cooling circuit. Battery temperature rises to 45 °C (manufacturer limit: 40 °C). Fault code **P0A93 — High Voltage Battery Pack Temperature** is stored. The charger automatically reduces charging rate to 20% of maximum.\n\n**Symptoms:** Battery over-temperature MIL active; charging slower than expected; electric water pump runs continuously.\n\n**Required:** (a) List FOUR (4) possible causes of the battery cooling failure in the liquid cooling circuit. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe the FIRST FOUR (4) diagnostic steps a technician should perform, in correct sequence. *(6 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 5 (10 marks)\n\n**System State:** Ambient temperature 35 °C. Driver operates HVAC on maximum cooling (set: 18 °C). Refrigerant high-side pressure reads 28 bar; low-side reads 1.2 bar. Normal high-side should be 16–22 bar at this ambient temperature.\n\n**Symptoms:** Cabin vent temperature 24 °C (should be 5–10 °C); compressor makes clicking/cycling noise every 30 seconds; no refrigerant leaks visible.\n\n**Required:** (a) Interpret the abnormal refrigerant pressures and identify the MOST LIKELY cause. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe the diagnostic procedure to confirm your diagnosis and state the corrective action. *(6 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 6 (10 marks)\n\n**System State:** A BEV parked overnight. Next morning, the driver notices a clear puddle under the front of the vehicle. Low Coolant Level warning illuminates on startup. Expansion tank level is at MIN mark.\n\n**Symptoms:** No fault codes stored; electric water pump runs normally; no steam from under bonnet; coolant appears colourless and clean.\n\n**Required:** (a) List the THREE (3) most likely sources of a BEV coolant leak in this scenario. *(3 marks)* (b) Describe the complete leak detection procedure using a coolant pressure tester, including: preparation, pressurising step, acceptable pressure hold time, and interpretation of results. *(7 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 7 (8 marks)\n\n**System State:** Battery temperature rises to 38 °C during highway driving (limit 40 °C). Technician connects scan tool and performs active test on the HV battery cooling water pump. Scan tool shows: Commanded = ON; Current draw = 0.2 A (normal: 4–8 A); No rotation detected.\n\n**Symptoms:** High battery temperature; active test fails; no coolant flow noise from battery circuit.\n\n**Required:** Diagnose whether the fault is in (a) the water pump itself, (b) the pump drive circuit (wiring/fuse), or (c) the Thermal Management ECU. For each, state ONE (1) specific test to confirm or eliminate it as the fault source. *(8 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 8 (8 marks)\n\n**System State:** After replacing the electric water pump on the battery cooling circuit, the technician must verify the repair is complete and the system is functioning correctly before returning the vehicle.\n\n**Required:** Describe the complete **cooling system functionality test** procedure after water pump replacement. Include: coolant refill, air bleeding, active test, temperature monitoring, and final verification steps. State the acceptable temperature range for the battery circuit at idle. *(8 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 9 (4 marks)\n\n**System State:** A technician is about to replace the electric compressor on a BEV. The vehicle uses R1234yf refrigerant.\n\n**Required:** State the mandatory refrigerant recovery procedure that must be completed BEFORE removing the compressor. Include: (a) the equipment used, (b) the reason for recovery (not venting), and (c) the HV safety step required for compressor removal. *(4 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 10 (10 marks)\n\n**System State:** A scan tool reveals THREE simultaneous fault codes on a BEV brought in for cooling complaints:\n- **P0A93** — Battery Pack Over-Temperature\n- **P0AAD** — Battery Coolant Pump A Circuit Low\n- **B1678** — HVAC Refrigerant Pressure Sensor Circuit\n\n**Symptoms:** Battery temperature: 42 °C; HVAC cabin temp: 26 °C (set: 22 °C); no coolant visible on ground.\n\n**Required:** (a) Explain how you would PRIORITISE these three fault codes for diagnosis and why. *(4 marks)* (b) Describe a systematic diagnostic approach to determine whether the faults are independent or related to a single root cause. *(6 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## Question 11 (10 marks)\n\n**System State:** A 5-year-old BEV (odometer: 95,000 km) is brought in for annual service. The owner reports that fast charging now causes the battery temperature to reach 38 °C, whereas it used to stabilise at 30–32 °C when the vehicle was new. No fault codes are stored.\n\n**Symptoms:** Gradual performance degradation of battery cooling; all cooling components function during active test; coolant level correct; electric water pump current normal.\n\n**Required:** (a) List THREE (3) possible causes of gradual cooling performance degradation in an aging BEV. *(3 marks)* (b) Describe the inspection procedure to identify the root cause, including coolant condition check, clogging inspection, and heat exchanger assessment. *(7 marks)*\n\n---\n\n## SKEMA PEMARKAHAN / MARKING SCHEME\n\n### Question 1 (10 marks — 3–4 marks per circuit; deduct for incomplete descriptions)\n\n| Circuit | Components | Temperature Range |\n|---------|-----------|-------------------|\n| Battery cooling | Traction battery pack | 15–35 °C (precisely controlled) |\n| Powertrain cooling | Motor, inverter, DC-DC converter | Up to 65 °C |\n| HVAC (cabin) | Condenser, evaporator, PTC heater | Per climate demand |\n\n**Rubric per circuit:** 4 = name + components + temp range correct; 3 = two elements correct; 2 = one element correct; 1 = vague answer.\n\n### Question 2 (10 marks — 2 marks each)\n\n(a) Electric water pump: circulates coolant independently of motor speed; can run stationary; **HV-powered** (battery/powertrain circuit) or **LV** (auxiliary).\n(b) PTC heater: electric resistance cabin heater, self-regulating, **HV-powered**, 3–8 kW.\n(c) 3-way valve: directs coolant between circuits; **LV** controlled by Thermal ECU.\n(d) Electric compressor: compresses refrigerant for HVAC cooling; **HV-powered**.\n(e) EXV: regulates refrigerant flow/pressure at evaporator inlet; **LV** (electronically controlled).\n\n### Question 3 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 6 marks:** Dielectric coolant prevents electrical short-circuit if coolant contacts HV components (2m). Mixing consequences: degraded dielectric properties leading to electrical conductivity risk (2m); possible corrosion/precipitation/gel from incompatible inhibitor packages (2m).\n**(b) 4 marks:** R1234yf = A2L (mildly flammable, low toxicity) (2m). Handling requirement: store away from ignition sources / use approved refrigerant recovery machine rated for A2L / no open flames in work area (2m).\n\n### Question 4 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 4 marks — 1 mark each:** Water pump failure; clogged coolant passage/heat exchanger; low coolant level (leak); thermostat/3-way valve stuck; degraded coolant (reduced thermal transfer).\n**(b) 6 marks — 1.5 marks each step:** (1) Read and record all fault codes; (2) Check coolant level in expansion tank; (3) Perform active test on battery cooling pump — verify current and flow; (4) Pressure test cooling circuit for leaks.\n\n### Question 5 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 4 marks:** High-side 28 bar = significantly above normal (16–22 bar) → over-charged system or blocked condenser (2m). Low-side 1.2 bar = normal/slightly low. Most likely: refrigerant overcharge OR condenser airflow blockage causing excessive head pressure (2m).\n**(b) 6 marks:** Confirm condenser fan operation (1m); inspect condenser for blockage (leaves, debris) (1m); if condenser clear, measure refrigerant charge vs. manufacturer specification by weight (2m); if overcharged, recover excess refrigerant using recovery machine until correct charge weight achieved (2m).\n\n### Question 6 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 3 marks — 1 each):** Coolant hose connection/fitting; expansion tank cap seal; heat exchanger (radiator) pinhole; water pump housing seal; coolant temperature sensor O-ring.\n**(b) 7 marks:** Connect pressure tester to expansion tank neck (1m); pressurise to manufacturer specification — typically 1.0–1.5 bar (2m); observe pressure gauge for 10 minutes (1m); pressure drop indicates leak present (1m); inspect all hoses, fittings, pump, heat exchangers systematically while pressurised (2m); UV dye can supplement if leak not visible.\n\n### Question 7 (8 marks — 2–3 marks each)\n\n(a) **Pump itself:** Disconnect pump connector → measure resistance across pump motor terminals; out-of-spec = pump failed internally (3m).\n(b) **Drive circuit:** With connector disconnected, measure voltage at supply terminal with pump commanded ON; 0V = blown fuse or open circuit wiring (3m).\n(c) **Thermal ECU:** If supply voltage present and pump resistance in spec but no rotation → pump seized; if no command signal output → ECU fault; verify ECU output with oscilloscope or scan tool live data (2m).\n\n### Question 8 (8 marks)\n\nRefill coolant to MAX level (1m) → bleed air per manufacturer procedure (gravity fill or vacuum fill tool) (2m) → connect scan tool and run active test on water pump; verify current 4–8A and flow audible (2m) → start vehicle in READY mode; monitor coolant temperatures via scan tool (1m) → battery circuit temperature should stabilise at 20–30 °C at idle (1m) → verify no pressure decay on pressure test and no fault codes (1m).\n\n### Question 9 (4 marks)\n\n(a) Equipment: refrigerant recycling/recovery machine rated for R1234yf (1m).\n(b) Reason: R1234yf is a greenhouse gas; venting is prohibited under environmental regulations; must be recovered for reuse or certified disposal (1m).\n(c) HV safety: perform full HV de-energisation and confirm zero-energy state before disconnecting any compressor electrical connector or refrigerant lines — compressor drive operates at HV (up to 400V DC) (2m).\n\n### Question 10 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 4 marks:** Prioritise P0AAD (pump circuit low) first — it is a root cause that directly leads to P0A93 (over-temp) (2m). B1678 (pressure sensor) is separate HVAC issue (1m). Correct prioritisation order: P0AAD → P0A93 (related) → B1678 (independent) (1m).\n**(b) 6 marks:** Diagnose P0AAD first: active test battery pump, check fuse and wiring (2m); if pump fault confirmed and repaired, re-scan — if P0A93 clears, faults were related (2m); diagnose B1678 independently: check refrigerant pressure sensor wiring, connector, and sensor output voltage (2m).\n\n### Question 11 (10 marks)\n\n**(a) 3 marks — 1 each:** Degraded coolant (reduced thermal conductivity after years of service); internal clogging of battery heat exchanger (scale/particulate buildup); reduced electric water pump performance (wear, reduced flow rate).\n**(b) 7 marks:** Coolant condition: check colour (should be clean, specified colour), pH with test strips (2m); clogging inspection: compare water pump flow rate (active test current draw vs. spec); check for particulate in coolant (2m); heat exchanger: inspect for external debris blockage; perform cooling circuit flush test; compare inlet vs. outlet temperature differential across heat exchanger — reduced differential indicates reduced heat transfer (3m).\n","numLines":191,"startLine":1,"totalLines":191}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01PMbM2rTiUa7x7G9qp1xX1m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. The BEV KA.md uses a format with `## Question N` headings, not `## QN` headings. But the test command says `grep -c '^## Q' aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md` - that would match both `## Question N` and `## QN`.\n\nNow, the story says to create the file at `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md`. But the actual directory is `aesthetic-services/C03/`. There's already a KA.md there. \n\n\nThe test commands are checking for 10 questions, at least 3 scenarios or case studies, and at least 30 total EN: and BM: terms in a file at `aesthetic-services/c03-manual-body-massage/KA.md` - a path that doesn't currently exist. I need to create that directory structure and populate the file accordingly. I need to structure the KA.md file with 10 questions covering anatomy identification, scenario-based technique selection, contraindication recognition, and safety procedures, each with question text, scenario context, expected answers, rubric points for different confidence levels, bilingual terms, and CoCU references.\n\nNow I'm calculating the bilingual term requirements — with the grep pattern counting individual \"EN:\" and \"BM:\" occurrences, I need at least 30 total matches, which means roughly 15 bilingual pairs across the document. Distributing 2-3 bilingual terms per question should get me to that threshold comfortably.\n\nI'm realizing the KP-02.md reference in the story points to a file structure that doesn't exist yet, and the current KP-02.md focuses on workspace preparation rather than anatomy. I'll need to draw on massage anatomy knowledge directly for the C03 questions, using the contraindications information from KP-01.md as a foundation. I need to structure this with 10 questions total—four anatomy identification questions covering different body regions, three scenario-based questions with clinical decision-making, and three more questions to meet the requirements. I'll weave in bilingual anatomy terminology using the EN:/BM: format throughout, making sure to hit the minimum of 30 term references while keeping the content clinically relevant to massage practice. I'm settling on the `## Q1:` format to match the regex pattern, then structuring each question with a metadata table showing the type, marks, and content sections for the question text, expected answer, and rubric criteria.\n\nNow I'm calculating how many bilingual term pairs I need across all questions—the anatomy and scenario questions will require more terms than the contraindication ones.\n\nGenerating markdown table... I'm checking which knowledge files contain anatomy content—KP-01 covers consultation and contraindications, KP-02 is workplace safety, and KP-03 focuses on client preparation, so I need to look at KP-04 and KP-05 for the anatomical details I'll need for the questions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4173,"cache_read_input_tokens":39879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4173},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"be4c835f-4956-412a-b353-e6eda149e7dc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01PMbM2rTiUa7x7G9qp1xX1m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bz4aEcbqaSyMgXtzuU9wBt","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-04.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4173,"cache_read_input_tokens":39879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4173},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"a672ff0d-70ae-4492-8d0a-8411ae7fbe2b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bz4aEcbqaSyMgXtzuU9wBt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(4/6)\n4\t**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n5\t**Work Activity 4:** Carry Out Manual Body Massage\n6\t**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Objectives (Objektif)\n11\t1. Describe the five classical massage movements and their effects.\n12\t2. Explain proper body mechanics for the massage therapist.\n13\t3. Describe rhythm, rate, pressure and continuity in massage.\n14\t4. Explain the massage flow sequence for a full-body massage.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Always check the consultation card and confirm contraindications verbally before commencing treatment. Adjust pressure incrementally — never apply deep pressure without client consent. Stop immediately if the client reports pain, dizziness, or discomfort.\n19\t\n20\t## 1.0 Massage Medium Selection\n21\t\n22\tSelect the appropriate oil or cream based on:\n23\t- Client's skin type\n24\t- Treatment goals (relaxation vs. deep tissue)\n25\t- Allergies (check consultation card)\n26\t- Duration of massage\n27\t\n28\tApply a small amount to hands; warm by rubbing palms together before applying to client.\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2.0 The Five Classical Massage Movements (Lima Pergerakan Urutan Klasik)\n33\t\n34\t### 2.1 Effleurage (Gelinciran)\n35\t- **Hand Position:** Whole palm flat on skin; fingers together; thumbs slightly apart. Fingertips lead on smaller areas (neck, forearm).\n36\t- **Technique:** Long, continuous gliding strokes following the contour of the body.\n37\t- **Direction:** Always **towards the heart** (centripetal) to aid venous return; return stroke is lighter and off-body or feather-light.\n38\t- **Pressure:** Light to medium (feathering to firm glide).\n39\t- **Effects:** Warms superficial tissue, spreads massage medium evenly, initiates relaxation response (parasympathetic activation), improves venous and lymphatic return.\n40\t- **Uses:** Opening and closing every body region; linking stroke between deeper techniques.\n41\t\n42\t### 2.2 Petrissage (Uli-uli / Ramas)\n43\t- **Hand Position:** Both hands alternate; lift and compress muscle bulk between thumb and fingers (picking up); or use both palms to wring tissue in opposite directions.\n44\t- **Sub-types:** *Picking up* (lift and squeeze), *wringing* (alternate hand pull), *rolling* (lift and roll tissue between fingers), *squeezing* (compress and release).\n45\t- **Pressure:** Medium to firm; work within client's comfort range.\n46\t- **Effects:** Increases local arterial and venous blood flow, promotes lymphatic drainage, breaks down minor adhesions, releases muscle tension, improves tissue extensibility (keanjalan tisu).\n47\t- **Uses:** Major muscle groups — trapezius, latissimus dorsi, gluteals, quadriceps, hamstrings, gastrocnemius.\n48\t\n49\t### 2.3 Tapotement / Percussion (Ketukan)\n50\t- **Hand Position:** Varies by sub-type — see table below.\n51\t- **Technique:** Rhythmic, bouncing, alternating hand strikes. Wrists stay loose; movement originates from the wrist, not the elbow.\n52\t\n53\t| Sub-type | Hand Position | Suitable Area |\n54\t|----------|--------------|---------------|\n55\t| Hacking (*mencincang*) | Ulnar border of loosely held hands, alternating | Back, thighs, calves |\n56\t| Cupping (*cawan*) | Cupped palm creating air pocket | Back, buttocks |\n57\t| Beating (*pukul*) | Loose fist, ulnar side | Large fleshy areas (buttocks, thighs) |\n58\t| Pounding (*tumbuk lembut*) | Closed fist, knuckle side | Gluteals only |\n59\t| Tapping (*ketuk jari*) | Fingertips only, light | Scalp, face, delicate areas |\n60\t\n61\t- **Pressure:** Light to medium; always rhythmic and even.\n62\t- **Effects:** Stimulates cutaneous nerve endings, increases local circulation, tones superficial muscles, invigorates and energises.\n63\t- **Avoid:** Bony prominences, spine, kidney area (posterior ribs), varicose veins, recent injuries.\n64\t\n65\t### 2.4 Vibration (Getaran)\n66\t- **Hand Position:** Fingertips or flat palm in contact with skin; arm muscles create rapid micro-tremors transmitted to tissue.\n67\t- **Types:** *Static vibration* (hold position; vibrate continuously for 5-10 sec) or *running vibration* (move along nerve pathway while vibrating).\n68\t- **Pressure:** Light — the effect is neurological, not mechanical.\n69\t- **Effects:** Soothes peripheral nerve endings, relieves localised pain, stimulates sluggish circulation, aids visceral peristalsis (abdomen).\n70\t- **Uses:** Along nerve pathways (sciatic, ulnar), solar plexus (abdomen), tight paraspinal muscles.\n71\t\n72\t### 2.5 Friction (Geseran)\n73\t- **Hand Position:** Thumb pad, fingertip pads, or knuckles. Tissue is *not* glided over — the skin and therapist contact point move together over the underlying structure.\n74\t- **Technique:** Deep, concentrated circular (around joints) or cross-fibre (across muscle belly) movements. No additional medium — or wipe medium away first.\n75\t- **Pressure:** Deep and focused; build gradually.\n76\t- **Effects:** Breaks down fibrous adhesions and scar tissue, increases localised hyperaemia (increased blood flow), promotes tissue remodelling, loosens tight joint capsules.\n77\t- **Uses:** Around knee/elbow/shoulder joints, along tendon insertions, on trigger points and tension nodules (*nodul ketegangan*).\n78\t\n79\t---\n80\t\n81\t## 3.0 Body Mechanics (Mekanik Badan Terapis)\n82\t\n83\tProper body mechanics prevent therapist injury and ensure effective massage delivery:\n84\t\n85\t| Principle | Application |\n86\t|-----------|-------------|\n87\t| **Stance** | Feet shoulder-width apart; one foot forward (walk stance) or parallel (horse stance) |\n88\t| **Knees** | Slightly bent; never locked |\n89\t| **Back** | Straight; lean from the hips, not the waist |\n90\t| **Shoulders** | Relaxed; not raised or hunched |\n91\t| **Arms** | Elbows slightly bent; wrists neutral (not flexed or extended) |\n92\t| **Weight transfer** | Use body weight, not arm strength, to apply pressure |\n93\t| **Couch height** | Adjusted so therapist can work with straight back and relaxed shoulders |\n94\t\n95\t---\n96\t\n97\t## 4.0 Rhythm, Rate, Pressure and Continuity\n98\t\n99\t| Element | Definition | Guidelines |\n100\t|---------|-----------|------------|\n101\t| **Rhythm** (*irama*) | Regularity and pattern of movements | Consistent, flowing; adjust to treatment goal (slow for relaxation, brisk for stimulation) |\n102\t| **Rate** (*kadar*) | Speed of movements | Slow for relaxation; faster for stimulation/tapotement |\n103\t| **Pressure** (*tekanan*) | Force applied to the tissue | Light for relaxation/effleurage; medium for petrissage; deep for friction. Always check with client. |\n104\t| **Continuity** (*kesinambungan*) | Maintaining unbroken contact | Hands should not lose contact with the client during the massage. Use one hand to maintain contact when re-applying medium. |\n105\t\n106\t---\n107\t\n108\t## 5.0 Massage Flow Sequence (Full Body)\n109\t\n110\tA standard full-body massage flow:\n111\t\n112\t**Prone (face down) — approximately 30 minutes:**\n113\t1. Back — effleurage, petrissage, tapotement, friction on tension areas\n114\t2. Gluteals (if appropriate) — petrissage, tapotement\n115\t3. Posterior legs (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage, tapotement\n116\t4. Feet (posterior)\n117\t\n118\t**Turn client to supine (face up) — approximately 30 minutes:**\n119\t5. Anterior legs (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage\n120\t6. Feet (anterior/plantar)\n121\t7. Arms and hands (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage\n122\t8. Abdomen (if appropriate) — gentle clockwise effleurage\n123\t9. Chest/decollete (if appropriate) — light effleurage\n124\t10. Neck and shoulders — effleurage, petrissage, friction\n125\t11. Scalp (optional)\n126\t\n127\tEnd with slow effleurage strokes to signal completion.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## 6.0 Monitoring Client Response\n132\t\n133\tThroughout the massage:\n134\t- Check pressure: \"Is the pressure comfortable?\"\n135\t- Observe for: facial grimacing, muscle guarding, verbal cues of discomfort.\n136\t- Adjust technique based on client feedback.\n137\t- Monitor for adverse reactions (dizziness, nausea, emotional response).\n138\t- Respond to any discomfort immediately.\n139\t\n140\t---\n141\t\n142\t## 7.0 Post-Massage Procedures\n143\t\n144\t7.1 Remove excess massage medium with warm towels.\n145\t7.2 Allow client to rest for 2-3 minutes.\n146\t7.3 Assist client to sit up slowly.\n147\t7.4 Offer water.\n148\t7.5 Usher client to change into own attire.\n149\t7.6 Return personal belongings.\n150\t7.7 Record massage results on consultation card.\n151\t\n152\t---\n153\t\n154\t## 8.0 Relevant Anatomy for Manual Body Massage (Anatomi Berkaitan)\n155\t\n156\t### 8.1 Major Muscle Groups by Body Region\n157\t\n158\t| Body Region | Key Muscles (*Otot Utama*) | Massage Technique Focus |\n159\t|-------------|--------------------------|------------------------|\n160\t| Back | Trapezius, latissimus dorsi, erector spinae, rhomboids | Effleurage, petrissage, friction (paraspinal) |\n161\t| Shoulders/Neck | Upper trapezius, levator scapulae, sternocleidomastoid | Petrissage, friction, vibration |\n162\t| Gluteals | Gluteus maximus, medius, minimus | Petrissage, tapotement |\n163\t| Posterior thigh | Hamstrings (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus) | Effleurage, petrissage |\n164\t| Anterior thigh | Quadriceps femoris (rectus femoris, vastus group) | Effleurage, petrissage |\n165\t| Lower leg | Gastrocnemius, soleus (*betis*) | Effleurage, petrissage, friction |\n166\t| Abdomen | Rectus abdominis, external/internal obliques | Gentle clockwise effleurage only |\n167\t| Arms | Biceps brachii, triceps brachii, forearm flexors/extensors | Effleurage, petrissage |\n168\t\n169\t### 8.2 Physiological Effects of Massage\n170\t\n171\t| System (*Sistem*) | Effect of Massage |\n172\t|-------------------|------------------|\n173\t| Circulatory (*peredaran darah*) | Dilates superficial capillaries (hyperaemia); increases venous return; reduces blood pressure |\n174\t| Lymphatic (*limfatik*) | Stimulates lymph flow; reduces oedema; supports immune function |\n175\t| Muscular (*otot*) | Reduces muscle tension and fatigue; improves extensibility; breaks down lactic acid accumulation |\n176\t| Nervous (*saraf*) | Activates parasympathetic response (rest-and-digest); reduces cortisol and adrenaline; releases endorphins |\n177\t| Integumentary (*integumen*) | Improves skin elasticity; increases sebaceous activity; removes dead epidermal cells |\n178\t| Skeletal (*rangka*) | Improves range of motion at joints; reduces periarticular stiffness |\n179\t\n180\t### 8.3 Key Anatomical Landmarks to Avoid or Work Around\n181\t\n182\t| Landmark | Location | Action |\n183\t|----------|----------|--------|\n184\t| Popliteal fossa (*lekuk lutut*) | Back of knee | Avoid direct compression — major vessels present |\n185\t| Femoral triangle | Upper inner thigh | Avoid deep pressure — femoral artery and nerve |\n186\t| Axilla (*ketiak*) | Armpit | Avoid deep pressure — brachial plexus, axillary vessels |\n187\t| Spine / vertebral column | Midline back | Never apply direct pressure on spinous processes |\n188\t| Kidney region | Posterior ribs, flank | Avoid tapotement directly over kidneys |\n189\t| Carotid triangle | Lateral neck | Avoid compression — carotid artery, jugular vein, vagus nerve |\n190\t\n191\t---\n192\t\n193\t## References\n194\t1. NOSS S960-002-3:2020 — Manual Body Massage (CoCU C03, WA 4)\n195\t2. Salvo, S.G. (2019). *Massage Therapy: Principles and Practice*. Elsevier.\n196\t3. Nordmann, L. (2018). *Beauty Therapy: The Foundations*. Cengage Learning.\n197\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"5dc31d69-e3fe-47e7-8539-789ab76b6a8c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:55:59.399Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-04.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(4/6)\n**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n**Work Activity 4:** Carry Out Manual Body Massage\n**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n\n---\n\n## Objectives (Objektif)\n1. Describe the five classical massage movements and their effects.\n2. Explain proper body mechanics for the massage therapist.\n3. Describe rhythm, rate, pressure and continuity in massage.\n4. Explain the massage flow sequence for a full-body massage.\n\n---\n\n> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Always check the consultation card and confirm contraindications verbally before commencing treatment. Adjust pressure incrementally — never apply deep pressure without client consent. Stop immediately if the client reports pain, dizziness, or discomfort.\n\n## 1.0 Massage Medium Selection\n\nSelect the appropriate oil or cream based on:\n- Client's skin type\n- Treatment goals (relaxation vs. deep tissue)\n- Allergies (check consultation card)\n- Duration of massage\n\nApply a small amount to hands; warm by rubbing palms together before applying to client.\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 The Five Classical Massage Movements (Lima Pergerakan Urutan Klasik)\n\n### 2.1 Effleurage (Gelinciran)\n- **Hand Position:** Whole palm flat on skin; fingers together; thumbs slightly apart. Fingertips lead on smaller areas (neck, forearm).\n- **Technique:** Long, continuous gliding strokes following the contour of the body.\n- **Direction:** Always **towards the heart** (centripetal) to aid venous return; return stroke is lighter and off-body or feather-light.\n- **Pressure:** Light to medium (feathering to firm glide).\n- **Effects:** Warms superficial tissue, spreads massage medium evenly, initiates relaxation response (parasympathetic activation), improves venous and lymphatic return.\n- **Uses:** Opening and closing every body region; linking stroke between deeper techniques.\n\n### 2.2 Petrissage (Uli-uli / Ramas)\n- **Hand Position:** Both hands alternate; lift and compress muscle bulk between thumb and fingers (picking up); or use both palms to wring tissue in opposite directions.\n- **Sub-types:** *Picking up* (lift and squeeze), *wringing* (alternate hand pull), *rolling* (lift and roll tissue between fingers), *squeezing* (compress and release).\n- **Pressure:** Medium to firm; work within client's comfort range.\n- **Effects:** Increases local arterial and venous blood flow, promotes lymphatic drainage, breaks down minor adhesions, releases muscle tension, improves tissue extensibility (keanjalan tisu).\n- **Uses:** Major muscle groups — trapezius, latissimus dorsi, gluteals, quadriceps, hamstrings, gastrocnemius.\n\n### 2.3 Tapotement / Percussion (Ketukan)\n- **Hand Position:** Varies by sub-type — see table below.\n- **Technique:** Rhythmic, bouncing, alternating hand strikes. Wrists stay loose; movement originates from the wrist, not the elbow.\n\n| Sub-type | Hand Position | Suitable Area |\n|----------|--------------|---------------|\n| Hacking (*mencincang*) | Ulnar border of loosely held hands, alternating | Back, thighs, calves |\n| Cupping (*cawan*) | Cupped palm creating air pocket | Back, buttocks |\n| Beating (*pukul*) | Loose fist, ulnar side | Large fleshy areas (buttocks, thighs) |\n| Pounding (*tumbuk lembut*) | Closed fist, knuckle side | Gluteals only |\n| Tapping (*ketuk jari*) | Fingertips only, light | Scalp, face, delicate areas |\n\n- **Pressure:** Light to medium; always rhythmic and even.\n- **Effects:** Stimulates cutaneous nerve endings, increases local circulation, tones superficial muscles, invigorates and energises.\n- **Avoid:** Bony prominences, spine, kidney area (posterior ribs), varicose veins, recent injuries.\n\n### 2.4 Vibration (Getaran)\n- **Hand Position:** Fingertips or flat palm in contact with skin; arm muscles create rapid micro-tremors transmitted to tissue.\n- **Types:** *Static vibration* (hold position; vibrate continuously for 5-10 sec) or *running vibration* (move along nerve pathway while vibrating).\n- **Pressure:** Light — the effect is neurological, not mechanical.\n- **Effects:** Soothes peripheral nerve endings, relieves localised pain, stimulates sluggish circulation, aids visceral peristalsis (abdomen).\n- **Uses:** Along nerve pathways (sciatic, ulnar), solar plexus (abdomen), tight paraspinal muscles.\n\n### 2.5 Friction (Geseran)\n- **Hand Position:** Thumb pad, fingertip pads, or knuckles. Tissue is *not* glided over — the skin and therapist contact point move together over the underlying structure.\n- **Technique:** Deep, concentrated circular (around joints) or cross-fibre (across muscle belly) movements. No additional medium — or wipe medium away first.\n- **Pressure:** Deep and focused; build gradually.\n- **Effects:** Breaks down fibrous adhesions and scar tissue, increases localised hyperaemia (increased blood flow), promotes tissue remodelling, loosens tight joint capsules.\n- **Uses:** Around knee/elbow/shoulder joints, along tendon insertions, on trigger points and tension nodules (*nodul ketegangan*).\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Body Mechanics (Mekanik Badan Terapis)\n\nProper body mechanics prevent therapist injury and ensure effective massage delivery:\n\n| Principle | Application |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Stance** | Feet shoulder-width apart; one foot forward (walk stance) or parallel (horse stance) |\n| **Knees** | Slightly bent; never locked |\n| **Back** | Straight; lean from the hips, not the waist |\n| **Shoulders** | Relaxed; not raised or hunched |\n| **Arms** | Elbows slightly bent; wrists neutral (not flexed or extended) |\n| **Weight transfer** | Use body weight, not arm strength, to apply pressure |\n| **Couch height** | Adjusted so therapist can work with straight back and relaxed shoulders |\n\n---\n\n## 4.0 Rhythm, Rate, Pressure and Continuity\n\n| Element | Definition | Guidelines |\n|---------|-----------|------------|\n| **Rhythm** (*irama*) | Regularity and pattern of movements | Consistent, flowing; adjust to treatment goal (slow for relaxation, brisk for stimulation) |\n| **Rate** (*kadar*) | Speed of movements | Slow for relaxation; faster for stimulation/tapotement |\n| **Pressure** (*tekanan*) | Force applied to the tissue | Light for relaxation/effleurage; medium for petrissage; deep for friction. Always check with client. |\n| **Continuity** (*kesinambungan*) | Maintaining unbroken contact | Hands should not lose contact with the client during the massage. Use one hand to maintain contact when re-applying medium. |\n\n---\n\n## 5.0 Massage Flow Sequence (Full Body)\n\nA standard full-body massage flow:\n\n**Prone (face down) — approximately 30 minutes:**\n1. Back — effleurage, petrissage, tapotement, friction on tension areas\n2. Gluteals (if appropriate) — petrissage, tapotement\n3. Posterior legs (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage, tapotement\n4. Feet (posterior)\n\n**Turn client to supine (face up) — approximately 30 minutes:**\n5. Anterior legs (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage\n6. Feet (anterior/plantar)\n7. Arms and hands (right then left) — effleurage, petrissage\n8. Abdomen (if appropriate) — gentle clockwise effleurage\n9. Chest/decollete (if appropriate) — light effleurage\n10. Neck and shoulders — effleurage, petrissage, friction\n11. Scalp (optional)\n\nEnd with slow effleurage strokes to signal completion.\n\n---\n\n## 6.0 Monitoring Client Response\n\nThroughout the massage:\n- Check pressure: \"Is the pressure comfortable?\"\n- Observe for: facial grimacing, muscle guarding, verbal cues of discomfort.\n- Adjust technique based on client feedback.\n- Monitor for adverse reactions (dizziness, nausea, emotional response).\n- Respond to any discomfort immediately.\n\n---\n\n## 7.0 Post-Massage Procedures\n\n7.1 Remove excess massage medium with warm towels.\n7.2 Allow client to rest for 2-3 minutes.\n7.3 Assist client to sit up slowly.\n7.4 Offer water.\n7.5 Usher client to change into own attire.\n7.6 Return personal belongings.\n7.7 Record massage results on consultation card.\n\n---\n\n## 8.0 Relevant Anatomy for Manual Body Massage (Anatomi Berkaitan)\n\n### 8.1 Major Muscle Groups by Body Region\n\n| Body Region | Key Muscles (*Otot Utama*) | Massage Technique Focus |\n|-------------|--------------------------|------------------------|\n| Back | Trapezius, latissimus dorsi, erector spinae, rhomboids | Effleurage, petrissage, friction (paraspinal) |\n| Shoulders/Neck | Upper trapezius, levator scapulae, sternocleidomastoid | Petrissage, friction, vibration |\n| Gluteals | Gluteus maximus, medius, minimus | Petrissage, tapotement |\n| Posterior thigh | Hamstrings (biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus) | Effleurage, petrissage |\n| Anterior thigh | Quadriceps femoris (rectus femoris, vastus group) | Effleurage, petrissage |\n| Lower leg | Gastrocnemius, soleus (*betis*) | Effleurage, petrissage, friction |\n| Abdomen | Rectus abdominis, external/internal obliques | Gentle clockwise effleurage only |\n| Arms | Biceps brachii, triceps brachii, forearm flexors/extensors | Effleurage, petrissage |\n\n### 8.2 Physiological Effects of Massage\n\n| System (*Sistem*) | Effect of Massage |\n|-------------------|------------------|\n| Circulatory (*peredaran darah*) | Dilates superficial capillaries (hyperaemia); increases venous return; reduces blood pressure |\n| Lymphatic (*limfatik*) | Stimulates lymph flow; reduces oedema; supports immune function |\n| Muscular (*otot*) | Reduces muscle tension and fatigue; improves extensibility; breaks down lactic acid accumulation |\n| Nervous (*saraf*) | Activates parasympathetic response (rest-and-digest); reduces cortisol and adrenaline; releases endorphins |\n| Integumentary (*integumen*) | Improves skin elasticity; increases sebaceous activity; removes dead epidermal cells |\n| Skeletal (*rangka*) | Improves range of motion at joints; reduces periarticular stiffness |\n\n### 8.3 Key Anatomical Landmarks to Avoid or Work Around\n\n| Landmark | Location | Action |\n|----------|----------|--------|\n| Popliteal fossa (*lekuk lutut*) | Back of knee | Avoid direct compression — major vessels present |\n| Femoral triangle | Upper inner thigh | Avoid deep pressure — femoral artery and nerve |\n| Axilla (*ketiak*) | Armpit | Avoid deep pressure — brachial plexus, axillary vessels |\n| Spine / vertebral column | Midline back | Never apply direct pressure on spinous processes |\n| Kidney region | Posterior ribs, flank | Avoid tapotement directly over kidneys |\n| Carotid triangle | Lateral neck | Avoid compression — carotid artery, jugular vein, vagus nerve |\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. NOSS S960-002-3:2020 — Manual Body Massage (CoCU C03, WA 4)\n2. Salvo, S.G. (2019). *Massage Therapy: Principles and Practice*. Elsevier.\n3. Nordmann, L. (2018). *Beauty Therapy: The Foundations*. Cengage Learning.\n","numLines":197,"startLine":1,"totalLines":197}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01PMbM2rTiUa7x7G9qp1xX1m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012WgyvUTi3rYR7DRxVQVYAp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-05.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4173,"cache_read_input_tokens":39879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4173},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"38f8ab42-eca7-44a2-a19c-a9381d9d6e92"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012WgyvUTi3rYR7DRxVQVYAp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(5/6)\n4\t**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n5\t**Work Activity 5:** Carry Out Post Manual Body Massage Advice\n6\t**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Objectives (Objektif)\n11\t1. Explain how to interpret and record client feedback after massage.\n12\t2. Describe after care advice specific to manual body massage.\n13\t3. Explain home care product recommendations for massage clients.\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Advise the client to seek medical attention if they experience unusual pain, swelling, or discomfort lasting more than 48 hours after treatment. Do not diagnose — refer to a qualified medical practitioner.\n18\t\n19\t## 1.0 Client Feedback Interpretation\n20\t\n21\tAfter massage, ask the client:\n22\t- How do they feel? (relaxed, refreshed, sore)\n23\t- Was the pressure appropriate?\n24\t- Were there any areas of discomfort?\n25\t- Do they have any questions?\n26\t\n27\tRecord feedback on consultation card. Common post-massage responses:\n28\t- Feeling relaxed and light\n29\t- Mild muscle soreness (normal; lasts 24-48 hours)\n30\t- Increased thirst\n31\t- Emotional release (occasional)\n32\t- Improved range of motion\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 2.0 After Care Advice\n37\t\n38\t| Advice | Rationale |\n39\t|--------|-----------|\n40\t| Drink plenty of water (8+ glasses) | Flush metabolic waste released from tissues |\n41\t| Rest if feeling drowsy | Allow body to heal and process |\n42\t| Avoid strenuous exercise for 24 hours | Muscles need recovery time |\n43\t| Mild muscle soreness is normal (24-48 hours) | Tissue response to deep work |\n44\t| Take a warm (not hot) bath if desired | Soothes muscles without overstimulating |\n45\t| Avoid heavy meals immediately after | Body is in relaxation/recovery mode |\n46\t| Note any areas of continued discomfort | Report at next session |\n47\t\n48\t---\n49\t\n50\t## 3.0 Home Care Product Advice\n51\t\n52\t| Product | Purpose |\n53\t|---------|---------|\n54\t| Body moisturiser | Maintain skin softness from massage oil benefits |\n55\t| Muscle rub/balm (*balsam otot*) | For areas of residual tension |\n56\t| Epsom salt (for bath) | Magnesium absorption; muscle relaxation |\n57\t| Stretching exercises | Maintain flexibility gained from massage |\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## 4.0 Recording and Scheduling\n62\t\n63\t- Update consultation card with: treatment performed, areas worked, medium used, client response, after care advice given.\n64\t- Schedule next appointment based on service plan.\n65\t- Provide appointment confirmation.\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## References\n70\t1. NOSS S960-002-3:2020 — Manual Body Massage (CoCU C03, WA 5)\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a84c7db-e2bc-4d04-8952-637ff4edf9ad","uuid":"eac60038-fc45-40fe-bc53-5bd8d1968d06","timestamp":"2026-04-16T16:55:59.417Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\noss-to-wim\\aesthetic-services\\C03\\KP-05.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod / Code:** S960-002-3:2020-C03/KP(5/6)\n**Competency Unit:** C03 — Manual Body Massage\n**Work Activity 5:** Carry Out Post Manual Body Massage Advice\n**Paper Colour / Warna Kertas:** WHITE / PUTIH\n\n---\n\n## Objectives (Objektif)\n1. Explain how to interpret and record client feedback after massage.\n2. Describe after care advice specific to manual body massage.\n3. Explain home care product recommendations for massage clients.\n\n---\n\n> **AMARAN / WARNING:** Advise the client to seek medical attention if they experience unusual pain, swelling, or discomfort lasting more than 48 hours after treatment. Do not diagnose — refer to a qualified medical practitioner.\n\n## 1.0 Client Feedback Interpretation\n\nAfter massage, ask the client:\n- How do they feel? (relaxed, refreshed, sore)\n- Was the pressure appropriate?\n- Were there any areas of discomfort?\n- Do they have any questions?\n\nRecord feedback on consultation card. Common post-massage responses:\n- Feeling relaxed and light\n- Mild muscle soreness (normal; lasts 24-48 hours)\n- Increased thirst\n- Emotional release (occasional)\n- Improved range of motion\n\n---\n\n## 2.0 After Care Advice\n\n| Advice | Rationale |\n|--------|-----------|\n| Drink plenty of water (8+ glasses) | Flush metabolic waste released from tissues |\n| Rest if feeling drowsy | Allow body to heal and process |\n| Avoid strenuous exercise for 24 hours | Muscles need recovery time |\n| Mild muscle soreness is normal (24-48 hours) | Tissue response to deep work |\n| Take a warm (not hot) bath if desired | Soothes muscles without overstimulating |\n| Avoid heavy meals immediately after | Body is in relaxation/recovery mode |\n| Note any areas of continued discomfort | Report at next session |\n\n---\n\n## 3.0 Home Care Product Advice\n\n| Product | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| Body moisturiser | Maintain skin softness from massage oil benefits |\n| Muscle rub/balm (*balsam otot*) | For areas of residual tension |\n| Epsom salt (for bath) | Magnesium absorption; muscle relaxation |\n| Stretching exercises | Maintain flexibility gained from massage |\n\n---\n\n## 4.0 Recording and Scheduling\n\n- Update consultation card with: treatment performed, areas worked, medium used, client response, after care advice given.\n- Schedule next appointment based on service plan.\n- Provide appointment confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## References\n1. NOSS S960-002-3:2020 — Manual Body Massage (CoCU C03, WA 5)\n","numLines":71,"startLine":1,"totalLines":71}}}
